                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: read the team and the risk`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is the diagnosis week. The Atlas Core squad's intrinsic-motivation index has fallen from <strong>82 to 71</strong> on the monitoring rumour, two routing experts have updated their LinkedIn, and the board wants “visibility and control.” Before you touch a single lever, separate <em>real</em> delivery risk from <em>manufactured</em> risk. Using Self-Determination Theory's three needs — autonomy, competence, relatedness — map which of the board's proposed controls threatens which need, and be honest about the <strong>≈12% (800-hour) gap</strong> that no amount of pressure can close.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Plan: design the pay & recognition scheme`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Decide how — and whether — to reward the Nordfrakt effort. The board's <strong>NOK 60 000/engineer</strong> bonus is paid only if 1 December is hit (660 000 kr in full). Kuvaas's central finding: strong performance-contingent pay can <em>crowd out</em> the intrinsic motivation that complex knowledge work depends on. The same money framed as “we trust you, here's a share of the upside” lands very differently from “hit the date or forfeit it.” And remember — no bonus creates 800 hours out of thin air. Decide the scheme, its <em>framing</em>, and how far you act on the real gap.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Decide: set the monitoring & autonomy dials`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The core decision. Set three dials on a 0–10 scale: <strong>monitoring intensity</strong>, <strong>autonomy granted</strong>, and <strong>goal & transparency structure</strong>. The model returns the inverted-U: too little structure and the deadline drifts; too much control and motivation, trust and velocity collapse <em>together</em>. Real-time activity surveillance makes engineers optimise for visible activity, not for solving the routing problem. And the routing knowledge sits in <strong>two heads</strong> — decide whether you manage the average or the two people who actually determine whether you deliver.`,
