                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_surveillance:  `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> Real-time monitoring attacks <em>autonomy</em>; the deadline-tied bonus signals distrust and crowds out the intrinsic drive your routing experts run on. Naming which lever harms which need is exactly how you avoid de-risking delivery by manufacturing the risk.`,
                    coach_r1_logging:       `<strong>The logging is the symptom, not the disease.</strong> Daily time-sheets annoy people, but it's the <em>surveillance</em> and the contingent bonus that do the deep damage — one to autonomy, one to intrinsic drive. Treat the visible bureaucracy as the whole problem and you'll leave the real harm in place.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Just nerves” is how a high-autonomy team quietly empties out.</strong> The index already fell 11 points on a <em>rumour</em> — that is identity and autonomy threat, not jitters. Underestimate it now and you'll be doing retention by exit interview.`,
                    coach_r1_gapreal:       `<strong>Honest, and rare under pressure.</strong> 7 200 hours needed, ~6 400 available — the 800-hour gap is structural. Naming it means you'll close it with focus, scope or capacity instead of pretending a bonus can conjure the hours.`,
                    coach_r1_gappressure:   `<strong>“The right pressure will find the hours” is the planning dodge.</strong> No bonus creates 800 hours out of thin air. You're about to use motivation levers to paper over a capacity decision — and the math won't move because you wished at it.`,
                    coach_r1_gapdenial:     `<strong>“No real gap, the estimate is padded” is the most expensive sentence in the room.</strong> The internal estimate is 7 200 hours against ~6 400 — deny it now and the deadline finds you out in month seven, when there's no time left to add capacity.`,
                    coach_r1_tighten:       `<strong>Tightening the screws on day one is the control trap.</strong> Before anyone trusts you, surveillance reads as “we don't believe you'll deliver.” Motivation drops, the two experts move closer to the door, and you've made the deadline <em>less</em> likely while feeling safer. What did the control actually buy?`,
                    coach_r1_refuse:        `<strong>Trust-based doesn't mean blind.</strong> Refusing the board any signal ignores a real 400 000 kr/week penalty and a 6-week walk-away clause. There's a difference between surveillance of activity and transparency on outcomes — give them the second, not nothing.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named that monitoring and the contingent bonus do the real damage, owned the structural hours gap, and opened by offering outcome transparency instead of surveillance. That's the posture that keeps trust intact while still giving the board a credible signal.`,
                    coach_r2_contingent:    `<strong>The full NOK 60 000 contingent bonus, adopted uncritically — the crowding-out trap.</strong> Your two senior engineers already earn well above market and are driven by the algorithm. One just told the product owner, “So now I only matter if we hit a date someone else set?” The 660 000 kr bought resentment, not motivation.`,
                    coach_r2_teambonus:     `<strong>A team-based bonus decoupled from individual output is far safer.</strong> It rewards the collective effort without pitting people against each other or making the money feel like a leash. Less crowding-out than the contingent scheme — though recognition and closing the gap matter more than the cash.`,
                    coach_r2_recognition:   `<strong>Non-contingent recognition and investment is the trust signal.</strong> Conference budget, a learning sprint, public credit — these say “we value you” without making the next paycheck conditional on a date someone else set. For intrinsically driven knowledge workers, this protects the drive the work depends on.`,
                    coach_r2_reinvest:      `<strong>Reinvesting the 660 000 kr into a third squad confronts the real problem.</strong> A bonus can't create hours; capacity can. You've started closing the structural gap instead of paying people to feel the pressure. Just don't forget the motivation question entirely — the existing squad still needs a trust signal.`,
                    coach_r2_reframe:       `<strong>Same money, different meaning.</strong> Reframing the contingent bonus as a share of the upside — “we trust you to solve this, here's a stake” — turns a controlling signal into an informational one. That's Kuvaas's point exactly: what changed wasn't the amount, it was the meaning.`,
                    coach_r3_surveillance:  `<strong>You cranked monitoring to real-time dashboards and daily logging.</strong> Intrinsic motivation just fell and effective velocity <em>dropped</em> — engineers are now optimising for visible activity, not for solving the routing problem. You wanted the deadline more likely; it's now less likely. What did the surveillance actually buy you?`,
                    coach_r3_abdication:    `<strong>Full autonomy with no shared goal isn't trust — it's abdication.</strong> You told the squad “you're trusted, figure it out” and walked away. Three weeks in, two engineers are gold-plating a visualisation nobody asked for and the critical routing path has slipped. How do you give direction without taking control?`,
                    coach_r3_control:       `<strong>Assigning every task and sequence is control by another name.</strong> A squad that self-organised for years now waits to be told what to do — motivation falls, the experts feel managed-down, and you've thrown away the very autonomy that made them productive.`,
                    coach_r3_enabling:      `<strong>That's the enabling band of the inverted-U.</strong> Light, outcome-based monitoring; real autonomy over <em>how</em> the work gets done; a co-owned goal with full transparency on the constraint. Motivation, trust and velocity rise together — clear shared direction without surveillance.`,
                    coach_r3_concentration: `<strong>Your average looks fine — but the tail will sink you.</strong> Routing knowledge sits in two heads, both near 40% flight risk. Manage the mean and you can lose one in week seven; the deadline is gone regardless of dashboards. Are you managing a number, or the two people who decide whether you deliver?`,
                    coach_r4_rebuild:       `<strong>Rebuilding trust before reaching for control is the move.</strong> A counter-offer and knowledge-transfer for the leaver, then an honest capacity re-plan with the squad — that's how you respond to a setback without manufacturing a second one. Coercion here would have finished the job the rumour started.`,
                    coach_r4_coerce:        `<strong>Doubling down on control after the setback is the manufactured failure, complete.</strong> More dashboards and a threatened bonus forfeit when one expert has already gone — motivation collapses, the second expert follows, and velocity falls further. The deadline didn't kill the project; the response to the deadline did.`,
                    coach_r4_absorb:        `<strong>Silently absorbing 600 extra hours is the planning dodge under fire.</strong> Telling the team to “just push through” a 600-hour scope add without renegotiating is how good people burn out and quietly leave. The gap is now bigger, and you've still not named it to Nordfrakt.`,
                    coach_r4_rescope:       `<strong>Renegotiating honestly is the accountable move.</strong> Phasing the +600 hours and agreeing a realistic 1 Dec scope keeps the relationship <em>and</em> the team intact — and names what added capacity would cost to hit the full scope. Trust and accountability coexist via outcome transparency.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Promising the full original-plus-new scope by 1 Dec is a promise you can't keep.</strong> Protecting the relationship by never raising the gap just defers the broken promise to December, when the 400 000 kr/week penalty and the walk-away clause are live. Honesty now is cheaper than a surprise then.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's the brief a board trusts.</strong> You named what the trust-based model bought, what the structural gap really costs, and a defensible path to the date — without chasing one dashboard number or blaming the team. You ran the operating model deliberately, and the four KPIs show it.`,
                    coach_r4_reassure:      `<strong>“The dashboards show we're on track” invites the obvious probe.</strong> Activity metrics aren't delivery, and downplaying the flight risk while a key engineer is already half-out is how boards stop trusting their managers. Lead with the real position, not the comforting one.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the team is the manager abdicating the job.</strong> The under-delivery you're describing was the predictable result of the controls — “if they'd worked harder” misreads knowledge work entirely. The team wasn't the problem; how you led them under pressure was the test.`,

